CAT = "caguardForce"; SEG = "FOR"
# Syllabus section 11: use of force theory.
Q = [
("Under section 25 of the Criminal Code, how much force may a person authorized to act use?",
 "As much as is necessary", ["Any force the client wants", "Double the force used on you", "None at any time"],
 "Section 25 protects a person acting on reasonable grounds who uses as much force as is necessary for the purpose. Anything beyond that is not protected."),
("Under the Criminal Code, what happens if you use excessive force?",
 "You are criminally responsible", ["Your employer takes all of the blame", "Nothing, if you were on duty", "Only your licence is affected"],
 "Section 26 makes anyone who uses excessive force criminally responsible for the excess. Being on duty is no defence."),
("To reduce the risk of positional asphyxia during a restraint, you should:",
 "Keep weight off their back", ["Keep them face down longer", "Kneel on their chest", "Sit on them until calm"],
 "Pressure on the back or chest of someone lying face down can stop them breathing. Get them onto their side or sitting up as soon as it is safe."),
("A person is sweating heavily, stripping off clothes in the cold, shouting and very strong. This suggests:",
 "A medical emergency", ["Normal intoxication", "A planned robbery", "A harmless prank"],
 "These signs, often taught as excited delirium, can end in sudden death, especially during a struggle. Call 911, avoid agitating them and avoid restraint if possible."),
("In a use of force model, what sits at the centre and is done constantly?",
 "Assessing the situation", ["Physical control", "Your presence", "Lethal force"],
 "Canadian use of force models, such as the National Use of Force Framework, put ongoing assessment of the situation at the centre: you keep reassessing and adjust your response as the behaviour changes."),
("What is the lowest level of response in a use of force model?",
 "Presence", ["Soft physical control", "Intermediate weapons", "Hard physical control"],
 "A uniformed guard's presence alone often deters or calms. Communication is the next step, before any physical control."),
("A person goes limp and refuses to move, but does not fight. Their behaviour is:",
 "Passive resistant", ["Active resistant", "Assaultive", "Cooperative"],
 "Passive resistance is non-compliance without physical effort against you, such as going limp. Pulling away or bracing would be active resistance."),
("A person pulls away and braces against you to avoid being held. Their behaviour is:",
 "Active resistant", ["Passive resistant", "Cooperative", "Grievous bodily harm"],
 "Active resistance uses physical effort to resist control without attacking you. Striking at you would make it assaultive."),
("Whether your force was reasonable is judged by:",
 "What a reasonable person would see", ["How you felt afterwards", "The client's opinion of what happened", "How badly the person was injured"],
 "Courts ask what a reasonable person in your position, with what you knew at the time, would have done. Hindsight and your feelings are not the test."),
("When must you reduce or stop using force?",
 "As soon as resistance stops", ["When the person apologizes", "When police tell you to", "When your shift ends"],
 "Force is justified only while it is necessary. Once resistance stops, continued force becomes excessive."),
("Why try communication before physical control?",
 "It often ends it without injury", ["It is quicker than force", "It is what the client always requires", "It avoids writing a report"],
 "Most incidents are resolved by words. Communication reduces injuries to everyone and the legal risk of a physical response."),
("After handcuffing someone, what should you check?",
 "That they are not too tight", ["That they are loose enough to slip", "That the person is face down", "That the key is left in them"],
 "Check the fit and double-lock the cuffs so they do not tighten and injure the wrists. Then sit the person up to protect their breathing."),
("Can a licensed guard in Ontario carry pepper spray on duty?",
 "No, it is a prohibited weapon", ["Yes, with a licence", "Yes, if the client agrees", "Yes, on night shifts only"],
 "Pepper spray meant for use on people is a prohibited weapon under Canadian law, so guards may not carry it. Guards carry only equipment their employer lawfully issues."),
("Under the Criminal Code, self-defence requires that what you do is:",
 "Reasonable in the circumstances", ["Exactly equal to the force used on you", "Done only after being hit", "Approved by a manager"],
 "Section 34 requires a reasonable belief that force is being used or threatened, a defensive purpose, and an act that is reasonable in the circumstances. Exact matching of force is not the test."),
("A person in peaceable possession of property may use force to stop someone taking it if the force is:",
 "Reasonable", ["Deadly if needed", "Delayed until it is gone", "Used only with police"],
 "Section 35 allows a reasonable act to prevent someone taking or damaging property in your peaceable possession. Protecting property alone will not justify force likely to kill or seriously injure."),
("After you have used force, what should you do?",
 "Report it in full detail", ["Leave it out of your notes", "Mention it only if asked", "Wait to see if they complain"],
 "Record and report every use of force: what happened, why, what force you used and any injuries. Full notes protect you if the incident is reviewed."),
]
